            "q": "Read the article about sharing communities. Sharing Communities Sharing communities are on the increase. In fact, they are becoming increasingly common worldwide. A sharing community is simply one where people put things like their ideas or skills together to bring benefit to the whole community. Globally, people in cities and towns are thinking of ways to share everything from clothes to boats and holiday homes. As well as the groups who share on a very basic level, there are also groups that associate themselves with the ‘big-picture’ sharing movement. In other words, for these groups the emphasis is on things such as education and community building and promoting a general move towards sharing more widely. From neighbourhood-level groups to global organizations, these groups work to make sharing a normal part of life. They see sharing as a new concept; a way of achieving a means to a more democratic society. The basic idea behind the sharing community is that people pool resources, so that individuals get what they need, when they need it, and sell what they don’t use. The idea of ‘team consumption’ as a way of boosting the economy is, at least in part anyway, shared by governments, with some announcing tax cuts for individuals who invest in such social projects. In the UK rural villages have benefitted, and not only financially. St Briavels, a small village in Gloucestershire, England, has made considerable financial gains due to its energetic approach to sharing. The village’s 500 kW power-source unit, which everyone contributed money towards and which can power up to 1,000 homes, generates a profit for everyone involved, with £8,000 raised from sales of extra energy. Andrew Clarke, the director of the Resilient Energy company, is impressed by the results the project has led to in the village. According to Clarke, there is a much greater feeling of community among the villagers as people are interacting with each other more than they were before. On a much larger scale, Ouishare is an international network of business people, citizens, journalists and designers, among others, who share their professional ideas and who are working towards the development of a joint community and economy. It has centres in Paris, London, Berlin, Barcelona, Rome and Brussels. Antonin Leonard, one of the owners of Ouishare, explains that it represents a change in culture as it focuses on the people themselves and the chance the network gives them to voice their opinions, be their own bosses and generally change their lives completely. Leonard also emphasises that one of the central principles behind Ouishare is that it’s about people who actually do things rather than people who just discuss doing things. With a slightly different focus to Ouishare, Finland- based Sharetribe is a network of web-based communities that share goods, services, lifts, spaces such as empty rooms in a house and much more. The organization aims to encourage sharing in an environment of trust. As one of its owners, Juho Makkonen, puts it, the idea of building a community is one at the heart of Sharetribe, and as the world has always operated like this, it’s not in fact an entirely original idea. Makkonen explains that people naturally find it difficult to trust others but communities help us build up trust in people through the communication that’s developed. And not only that, but once people feel that they belong to a certain community, they want to do what they can to work for the good of that community. So, while it’s easy to imagine that the ‘sharing community’ is just another passing trend, it seems to be genuinely catching on, and on a global scale. It looks like it may be around for a while. B2   Progress test 2A Units 4–6 Complete the summary with a word from the box. There are three words that you do not need to use. concept   education   global   individuals means   organization   resources technology   trend There are more sharing communities around than ever before, but what exactly is a sharing community? The central __________ behind them is that people put their combined __________ together for the good of the community as a whole. This may involve sharing items such as clothes, or services such as __________. Many people believe that sharing communities are one __________ of developing democracy in society. And thanks to developments in __________, it’s possible to go beyond having small local sharing communities and become involved on a __________scale with networks based in several countries.: (3) Complete the sentence or answer the question.",
