            "id": 5,
            "num": 5,
            "exerciseNum": 1,
            "exerciseTitle": "Exercise 1: Read the blog post in which five people talk about",
            "q": "has the opportunity to learn a foreign language ____ A Kristina When I left school, all I knew was that I was really keen on art, so I jumped at the chance to work as an assistant in a small art gallery. But it turned out that all I had to do was make lists and keep things tidy. I didn’t exactly feel challenged! Nowadays I’m working with a firm of designers. Soon after I took up employment with them, they opened a shop near my home, which was an added bonus as it takes me only ten minutes to drive there as opposed to almost an hour in the past. I certainly don’t miss being stuck in traffic every morning. I use the time I save to work the odd evening or weekend if I’m needed; I really don’t mind doing that. B Mandeep Well, a job wo uldn’t suit me if it didn’t involve computers or programming languages in a big way. I’ve always worked in this field, in one role or another. I set up my own business last year. I’m what you might call a PC doctor and I sort out everyone’s problems. I could find myself working at any time. But strangely enough, this is quite a plus. Even though I’m on ‘standby’, I can meet up with old friends when I’m free during the day, or even go to the gym when it’s quiet. C Alannah I was told that the route to roles in hotel management started with restaurant work, though personally I found it all hard going – long hours, and little chance of promotion , which didn’t exactly motivate me. Anyway, I work as a receptionist now at a conference centre in the countryside, though not too far from home. I discovered only after I was a week into the job that it’s part of a worldwide chain and there are plenty of chances for staff exchanges, so I’ll get to use my language skills. Meanwhile, staff can take advantage of the beautiful woods and hills around the centre. D Kostas Because my family run a small business, when I left university most people thought I would just walk into a job. But I had other ideas which involved foreign travel and adventure. I started working for an international bank and it was OK to start with, but things turned out to be a bit slow, so I did end up joining the family business after all. Then Dad offered to pay for a college course in Spanish as we buy a lot of our supplies from South America. And I’m living at home, too, which is saving me a fortune on rent and bills! E Marla I’m an estate agent and our office is right in the centre of a lovely little market town. I feel really comfortable working there and can’t imagine being anywhere else. I was n’t always in the property business; I started out in sales and that’s a whole other world. I worked in one of those huge out-of-town shopping parks and it was tough as we had targets to meet every week. That led to lots of competition between staff and I soon realized that it wasn’t for me. Anyway, now I’m much happier and with the inside knowledge I’ve got here, I’ve been able to buy a small reasonably-priced apartment – my first home! B2 Unit test 7A",
